export default [
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company runs hundreds of AWS Lambda functions and suspects many are configured with more memory than they need. Which service analyzes their utilization history and recommends better memory sizes?","o":["AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Budgets","AWS Compute Optimizer","Amazon CloudWatch"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Compute Optimizer uses machine learning on CloudWatch utilization metrics to recommend optimal memory sizes for Lambda functions (and sizes for EC2, EBS, and more). CloudWatch collects the metrics but does not produce sizing recommendations."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"An operations team wants recommendations on whether its Amazon EBS volumes should change volume type, size, or provisioned IOPS based on how they are actually used. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Compute Optimizer","AWS Backup","Amazon Inspector","AWS Config"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Compute Optimizer analyzes EBS volume utilization and recommends a better volume type, size, or IOPS/throughput. AWS Config only records configuration changes and evaluates rules; it does not recommend right-sizing."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants recommendations for the best instance types for its Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling groups, together with a performance risk rating for each recommendation. Which service provides this?","o":["AWS Cost Explorer","AWS Health Dashboard","AWS Well-Architected Tool","AWS Compute Optimizer"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Compute Optimizer recommends EC2 instance types for Auto Scaling groups and rates the performance risk of each option. The AWS Well-Architected Tool reviews a workload against best-practice questions, not per-resource sizing."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"Which statements about AWS Compute Optimizer are correct? (Choose TWO.)","o":["It uses machine learning to analyze historical utilization metrics from Amazon CloudWatch","It can recommend right-sized resources for Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon EBS volumes, and AWS Lambda functions","It automatically resizes EC2 instances without any user action","It purchases Reserved Instances on the customer's behalf","It forecasts the next 12 months of the account's total AWS bill"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Compute Optimizer analyzes CloudWatch utilization history with machine learning and recommends sizes for EC2, Auto Scaling groups, EBS, Lambda, and more; the customer decides whether to apply them. Bill forecasting is a feature of AWS Cost Explorer, not Compute Optimizer."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company's EC2 instances are frequently CPU-constrained during peak hours. The team wants a service that flags these instances as under-provisioned and suggests larger instance types. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Service Catalog","AWS Compute Optimizer","Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","AWS License Manager"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Compute Optimizer classifies instances as over-provisioned, under-provisioned, or optimized and recommends instance types. EC2 Auto Scaling adds or removes instances but does not recommend a different instance type."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company has 200 licenses for a commercial database product. It wants to block new EC2 instances from launching with that software once all 200 licenses are in use. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Service Quotas","AWS License Manager","AWS Organizations","AWS Marketplace"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS License Manager lets you create license configurations with hard limits that prevent launches that would exceed your license count. Service Quotas manages AWS service limits, not third-party software license counts."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company brings its own Windows Server licenses that are bound to physical cores and wants AWS to automatically allocate and release Dedicated Hosts as instances launch, while tracking license usage. Which service helps with this?","o":["AWS Compute Optimizer","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","AWS Control Tower","AWS License Manager"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS License Manager works with host resource groups to automate Dedicated Host management for BYOL software and tracks license consumption. Compute Optimizer only recommends right-sized resources."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"An administrator wants to define license rules once in the management account and apply them to all accounts in the company's AWS Organizations organization, with a single view of license usage. Which service provides this?","o":["AWS License Manager","AWS Artifact","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Budgets"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS License Manager integrates with AWS Organizations so license configurations and usage can be managed centrally across accounts. AWS Artifact provides AWS compliance reports and agreements, not software license tracking."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"An auditor from a software vendor asks a company to prove how many licenses of its product are being used on AWS and on premises. Which AWS service helps the company track this usage to stay compliant?","o":["AWS CloudTrail","AWS Config","AWS License Manager","Migration Evaluator"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS License Manager tracks license usage across AWS and on-premises servers and reports it against your entitlements. CloudTrail records API calls; it does not count software license consumption."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A central IT team wants developers to launch only pre-approved, standardized environments (for example, an approved EC2 and RDS stack) through a self-service portal. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Marketplace","AWS Elastic Beanstalk","AWS Config","AWS Service Catalog"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Service Catalog lets administrators create portfolios of approved products (CloudFormation templates) that users can launch on their own. AWS Marketplace is a store of third-party software, not a catalog of an organization's own approved products."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants users to launch an approved product that creates IAM roles and VPC resources, without giving the users the IAM permissions to create those resources directly. Which service supports this with launch constraints?","o":["AWS CloudFormation","AWS IAM Identity Center","AWS Service Catalog","AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Service Catalog launch constraints let a product be provisioned with a specified IAM role, so end users only need permission to use the catalog. CloudFormation alone would require the user to have permissions for every resource in the template."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants to share a portfolio of approved infrastructure products with other AWS accounts in its organization and keep control of product versions centrally. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Service Catalog","AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)","AWS License Manager","AWS Systems Manager"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Service Catalog portfolios can be shared with other accounts or organizational units while the hub account manages product versions. AWS RAM shares resources such as subnets or transit gateways, not catalogs of deployable products."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"Which benefits does AWS Service Catalog provide? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Consistent governance by letting users deploy only approved IT services","Automatic patching of the operating systems of EC2 instances","Purchasing third-party software with billing on the AWS invoice","Recommendations to right-size underutilized resources","Self-service provisioning for end users from a curated list of products"],"a":[0,4],"e":"Service Catalog provides governed, self-service provisioning of approved products. Buying third-party software billed through AWS is AWS Marketplace, and patching is a Systems Manager capability."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company plans a product launch and needs to raise the maximum number of running On-Demand EC2 instances in its account. Where should it request this increase?","o":["AWS Trusted Advisor","Service Quotas","AWS Budgets","AWS Compute Optimizer"],"a":[1],"e":"Service Quotas lets you view your quotas and request increases from one place. Trusted Advisor can warn you when usage approaches a limit, but it cannot submit an increase request."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"An administrator wants a single place to view the default and applied quotas (formerly called limits) for many AWS services in an account. Which service should the administrator use?","o":["AWS Config","AWS Health Dashboard","Service Quotas","AWS Cost Explorer"],"a":[2],"e":"Service Quotas centrally shows default and applied quota values for AWS services and lets you request increases. AWS Config tracks resource configurations, not account quotas."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants every new account created in its AWS Organizations organization to automatically receive higher quotas for certain services. Which feature should it use?","o":["A Service Quotas quota request template","An AWS Budgets action","A service control policy (SCP)","An AWS Control Tower detective control"],"a":[0],"e":"A Service Quotas quota request template automatically submits predefined quota increase requests when new accounts are created in the organization. SCPs only restrict permissions; they cannot raise quotas."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A team wants to be alerted before its usage of an AWS resource reaches the account limit, so that a launch does not fail. Which combination supports this?","o":["AWS Budgets with a cost threshold","AWS CloudTrail with an S3 bucket","AWS Compute Optimizer with an enhanced metric","Service Quotas with an Amazon CloudWatch alarm on quota utilization"],"a":[3],"e":"Service Quotas integrates with CloudWatch so you can create alarms when usage approaches a quota value. AWS Budgets alerts on cost or usage spend, not on service limits."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants a dashboard that shows which of its many AWS accounts have resources that violate the organization's detective controls (guardrails). Which service provides this out of the box?","o":["AWS Service Catalog","AWS Control Tower","AWS Compute Optimizer","AWS License Manager"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Control Tower provides a dashboard of enrolled accounts and noncompliant resources detected by its controls. Service Catalog governs which products users can launch but does not report guardrail compliance."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"When AWS Control Tower sets up a landing zone, which shared accounts does it create for centralized logging and security auditing? (Choose TWO.)","o":["A Billing Conductor account","A Log Archive account","A Marketplace seller account","A Root user account for each Region","An Audit account"],"a":[1,4],"e":"The Control Tower landing zone creates a Log Archive account for centralized CloudTrail and Config logs and an Audit account for security and compliance teams. Separate billing or Marketplace accounts are not part of the standard landing zone."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"In AWS Control Tower, which type of control stops actions from happening, for example by preventing anyone from disabling AWS CloudTrail in member accounts?","o":["A detective control, implemented with AWS Config rules","A preventive control, implemented with service control policies","A proactive control, implemented with AWS Budgets","A corrective control, implemented with AWS Health Dashboard"],"a":[1],"e":"Preventive controls in Control Tower use SCPs to block disallowed actions. Detective controls use AWS Config rules to find noncompliant resources after they exist; they do not prevent actions."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company already has dozens of AWS accounts in AWS Organizations and wants to apply a standardized multi-account governance baseline with guardrails to them, instead of building it with custom scripts. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Well-Architected Tool","AWS Systems Manager","AWS Migration Hub","AWS Control Tower"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Control Tower can set up a landing zone in an existing organization and enroll existing accounts so they receive its controls. The Well-Architected Tool only reviews workloads against best practices; it does not enforce anything."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"An architect wants to document a workload by answering best-practice questions for each pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework and receive a list of high-risk issues with an improvement plan. Which service should the architect use?","o":["AWS Well-Architected Tool","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Config","AWS Control Tower"],"a":[0],"e":"The AWS Well-Architected Tool walks you through pillar questions, flags high-risk and medium-risk issues, and builds an improvement plan. AWS Trusted Advisor runs automated checks on account resources but does not review a workload's architecture through questions."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company reviews the same workload with the AWS Well-Architected Tool every quarter and wants to save a snapshot of each review to compare progress over time. Which feature should it use?","o":["Apply additional lenses in the AWS Well-Architected Tool","Enable detective controls in AWS Control Tower","Record milestones in the AWS Well-Architected Tool","Create a quota request template in Service Quotas"],"a":[2],"e":"Milestones record the state of a workload review at a point in time so you can track improvement. Lenses add sets of best-practice questions for specific technologies or industries; they do not save review history."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants to review its workloads in the AWS Well-Architected Tool against its own internal best practices in addition to the AWS pillars. What should it create?","o":["A custom AWS Config rule","An AWS Service Catalog portfolio","An AWS Control Tower preventive control","A custom lens in the AWS Well-Architected Tool"],"a":[3],"e":"The AWS Well-Architected Tool supports custom lenses so organizations can add their own questions and best practices to reviews. An AWS Config rule evaluates resource configurations, not architecture review questions."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A small company with no AWS Support plan beyond Basic wants a free, self-service way in the AWS Management Console to review its workloads against AWS architectural best practices. What should it use?","o":["AWS Professional Services","AWS Enterprise Support Technical Account Manager","AWS Well-Architected Tool","AWS Managed Services (AMS)"],"a":[2],"e":"The AWS Well-Architected Tool is available in the console at no additional charge for self-service workload reviews. Professional Services and a TAM can also help with architecture, but they are paid engagements."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"An EC2 instance in a company's account is scheduled for retirement because of degraded underlying hardware. Where will the company see a personalized notification about this event?","o":["AWS Health Dashboard","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Inspector"],"a":[0],"e":"The AWS Health Dashboard shows account-specific events, such as scheduled maintenance and instance retirements, that affect your resources. CloudTrail records API calls made in the account; it does not announce AWS-initiated events."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A company wants to automatically trigger a runbook whenever AWS reports an operational issue that affects resources in its account. Which combination should it use?","o":["AWS Config rules sent to AWS Budgets","AWS Health events sent to Amazon EventBridge","AWS Cost Explorer reports sent to Amazon SNS","Service Quotas sent to AWS Service Catalog"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Health publishes account-specific events to Amazon EventBridge, where rules can start automated actions. AWS Config rules evaluate resource configuration; they do not report AWS service events."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"A central operations team wants to see AWS Health events for all accounts in its AWS Organizations organization in one place. Which feature should it enable?","o":["Consolidated billing in AWS Organizations","Account Factory in AWS Control Tower","Organizational view in AWS Health Dashboard","Cross-account sharing in AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)"],"a":[2],"e":"The organizational view of AWS Health aggregates health events from all member accounts. Consolidated billing combines invoices but does not show service health events."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"What is the difference between the service health view and the account health view of the AWS Health Dashboard?","o":["Service health shows the general status of AWS services for everyone; account health shows events that affect your own resources","Service health is free; account health requires Enterprise Support","Service health covers only one Region; account health covers all Regions","Service health shows cost anomalies; account health shows security findings"],"a":[0],"e":"The service health page is public and shows the overall availability of AWS services, while your account health shows events that affect your specific accounts and resources. Neither view reports cost anomalies or security findings."},
  {"d":3,"t":"management","q":"Which service provides alerts and remediation guidance when AWS is experiencing events, such as an outage in an Availability Zone, that may affect the resources in a company's account?","o":["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Compute Optimizer","AWS Health Dashboard"],"a":[3],"e":"The AWS Health Dashboard gives proactive notifications and remediation guidance for AWS events that affect your account. Trusted Advisor checks your configuration against best practices; it does not report AWS-side incidents."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"Before a migration, a company needs to collect configuration, utilization, and network connection data from VMware virtual machines without installing software on each VM. Which option should it use?","o":["AWS Application Migration Service replication agents","AWS Application Discovery Service with the Agentless Collector","AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Application Discovery Service has an Agentless Collector that gathers VM data through VMware vCenter. Application Migration Service replicates servers for the actual move; it is not a discovery tool. (Application Discovery Service closed to new customers on November 7, 2025, but remains in scope for the exam.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A migration team needs to understand which on-premises servers talk to each other so it can group dependent applications into the same migration wave. Which service collects this dependency data?","o":["Migration Evaluator","AWS Application Discovery Service","AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)","AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Application Discovery Service agents collect network connection and process data that reveal server dependencies. Migration Evaluator focuses on building a cost business case, not detailed dependency mapping."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company wants to capture running processes and detailed performance data from physical on-premises servers that are not virtualized. What should it use?","o":["The AWS Application Discovery Service Agentless Collector for VMware vCenter","AWS Client VPN","AWS Backup","The AWS Application Discovery Service agent installed on each server"],"a":[3],"e":"The Discovery Agent runs on each physical or virtual server and collects system, process, and network data. The Agentless Collector works through VMware vCenter, so it cannot see physical servers."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"Which AWS services help a company plan a migration by gathering information about its existing on-premises environment? (Choose TWO.)","o":["AWS Application Migration Service","AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Application Discovery Service","Migration Evaluator"],"a":[3,4],"e":"AWS Application Discovery Service collects server inventory and dependency data, and Migration Evaluator collects usage data to build a cost business case. Application Migration Service performs the migration itself rather than the discovery."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company wants to rehost (lift and shift) 300 on-premises Windows and Linux servers to Amazon EC2 with minimal downtime and without changing the applications. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Application Migration Service","AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)","AWS Migration Hub"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Application Migration Service continuously replicates whole servers to AWS and launches them as EC2 instances at cutover. AWS DMS migrates databases only, not entire servers."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"Before cutting over migrated servers, a team wants to launch test instances on AWS while replication from the source servers continues, so it can validate the applications without affecting production. Which service supports this?","o":["AWS Backup","AWS Application Discovery Service","AWS Application Migration Service","Migration Evaluator"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Application Migration Service lets you launch non-disruptive test instances at any time while continuous replication keeps running. Application Discovery Service only collects data about servers; it does not replicate or launch them."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company wants to move virtual machines running in another public cloud to AWS using continuous block-level replication, and have them automatically converted to run natively on AWS. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS DataSync","AWS Storage Gateway","AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)","AWS Application Migration Service"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Application Migration Service replicates from physical, virtual, or other cloud servers and converts them to boot natively on EC2. DataSync copies files and objects, not whole bootable servers."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company wants to migrate on-premises servers to AWS and must be able to cut over with only minutes of downtime. Which features of AWS Application Migration Service make this possible? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Continuous block-level replication of the source servers","Conversion of database schemas between engines","A cost business case based on on-premises utilization","Launching cutover instances from up-to-date replicated data","Automatic rewriting of the application into microservices"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Application Migration Service keeps the replicated data current, so cutover only needs a short final sync and launch. Schema conversion is done by AWS SCT, and business cases come from Migration Evaluator."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A CFO wants a data-driven business case that projects the cost of running the company's current on-premises servers on AWS, based on actual utilization. Which service should the company use?","o":["AWS Compute Optimizer","AWS Cost Explorer","Migration Evaluator","AWS Application Migration Service"],"a":[2],"e":"Migration Evaluator analyzes on-premises utilization and produces a projected AWS cost business case with right-sized options. AWS Cost Explorer only analyzes costs that already occurred on AWS."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company wants to compare bring-your-own-license and license-included options for its Microsoft workloads as part of a migration business case. Which service models these licensing scenarios?","o":["Migration Evaluator","AWS License Manager","AWS Service Catalog","AWS Artifact"],"a":[0],"e":"Migration Evaluator models licensing options, such as BYOL versus license-included, in its migration business case. AWS License Manager tracks and enforces license usage after deployment; it does not build a migration cost case."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"Which service, formerly known as TSO Logic, collects on-premises usage data and produces a Quick Insights report showing estimated cost savings from moving to AWS?","o":["AWS Migration Hub","Migration Evaluator","AWS Application Discovery Service","AWS Well-Architected Tool"],"a":[1],"e":"Migration Evaluator (formerly TSO Logic) creates Quick Insights reports and detailed business cases about migration costs. Application Discovery Service gathers inventory and dependency data but does not estimate cost savings."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company in the early assess phase of a migration wants to right-size its future AWS environment and estimate the total cost of ownership before deciding whether to migrate. Which service fits best?","o":["AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","AWS Application Migration Service","Migration Evaluator","AWS Transit Gateway"],"a":[2],"e":"Migration Evaluator supports the assess phase with right-sizing and projected TCO for a migration decision. Application Migration Service is used later, to actually move the servers."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company is migrating applications with several tools at once, including AWS Application Migration Service and AWS Database Migration Service. Where can it track the progress of all these migrations in one place?","o":["AWS Migration Hub","AWS Health Dashboard","AWS Control Tower","AWS Trusted Advisor"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Migration Hub provides a single location to track migration status across multiple AWS and partner tools. The AWS Health Dashboard reports AWS service events, not migration progress. (Migration Hub closed to new customers on November 7, 2025, with AWS Transform as the recommended successor, but it remains in scope for the exam.)"},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A migration program manager wants to group servers into applications and see each application's migration status in a central dashboard. Which service provides this?","o":["AWS Service Catalog","Migration Evaluator","AWS Systems Manager","AWS Migration Hub"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Migration Hub lets you group discovered servers into applications and track the status of each application's migration. Migration Evaluator builds the cost business case but does not track migration progress."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"Data collected by AWS Application Discovery Service can be viewed and grouped in which AWS service to help plan and track migrations?","o":["AWS Config","AWS Migration Hub","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Compute Optimizer"],"a":[1],"e":"Application Discovery Service stores its data where AWS Migration Hub can use it to group servers and plan and track migrations. CloudWatch monitors AWS resources and does not hold on-premises discovery data."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"Which statements describe AWS Migration Hub? (Choose TWO.)","o":["It replicates server disks to AWS on its own","It provides a single place to discover existing servers, plan migrations, and track their status","It converts database schemas from Oracle to PostgreSQL","It integrates with migration tools such as AWS Application Migration Service and AWS Database Migration Service","It provides private connectivity between VPCs"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Migration Hub is a central place to discover, plan, and track migrations, and it gets status updates from integrated tools. The actual replication is done by tools such as Application Migration Service, and schema conversion by AWS SCT."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"Before migrating from Microsoft SQL Server to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL-Compatible Edition, a company wants a report showing how much of its schema can be converted automatically and which items need manual work. Which tool produces this?","o":["AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) ongoing replication","AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)","AWS Application Discovery Service","Migration Evaluator"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS SCT generates a database migration assessment report that lists what it can convert automatically and what needs manual effort. AWS DMS moves and replicates the data itself but does not assess schema conversion complexity."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"A company is moving its on-premises Teradata data warehouse to Amazon Redshift and needs to convert the data warehouse schema. Which tool should it use?","o":["AWS Glue Data Catalog","AWS Application Migration Service","Amazon Athena","AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS SCT converts data warehouse schemas, such as Teradata, Oracle, or Netezza, to Amazon Redshift. Application Migration Service rehosts servers without converting anything."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"Application code contains embedded SQL statements written for Oracle. The company is moving to Amazon Aurora MySQL-Compatible Edition. Which tool can help convert the SQL embedded in the application code?","o":["AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)","AWS CodeBuild","AWS Migration Hub","AWS Application Migration Service"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS SCT can scan application source code and convert embedded SQL to the target database dialect. AWS CodeBuild compiles and tests code but does not translate SQL dialects."},
  {"d":3,"t":"migration","q":"In which migration is the AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT) typically needed?","o":["A homogeneous migration from MySQL to Amazon RDS for MySQL","A rehost of web servers to Amazon EC2","A heterogeneous migration from Oracle to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL-Compatible Edition","Copying files from an NFS server to Amazon EFS"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS SCT is needed when the source and target engines differ, because the schema and code must be converted. In a homogeneous MySQL-to-MySQL migration, the schema is compatible and native tools or AWS DMS are enough."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company has 60 VPCs and two on-premises data centers. Managing a full mesh of VPC peering connections has become too complex. Which service provides a central hub to connect them all?","o":["AWS PrivateLink","AWS Client VPN","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Transit Gateway"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Transit Gateway acts as a regional hub that connects many VPCs and on-premises networks with transitive routing. PrivateLink exposes individual services privately; it does not route between whole networks."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"VPC A is peered with VPC B, and VPC B is peered with VPC C. Instances in VPC A cannot reach VPC C. Which service allows transitive routing between all three VPCs?","o":["An additional internet gateway","AWS Global Accelerator","AWS Transit Gateway","A NAT gateway in VPC B"],"a":[2],"e":"VPC peering is not transitive, but VPCs attached to AWS Transit Gateway can route to each other through the hub. A NAT gateway only provides outbound internet access for private subnets."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company wants a single AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection from its data center to reach dozens of VPCs, instead of creating a separate VPN to each VPC. What should it attach the VPN to?","o":["An AWS Transit Gateway","A virtual private gateway on each VPC","An Amazon Route 53 hosted zone","A VPC gateway endpoint"],"a":[0],"e":"Attaching the VPN to a transit gateway lets one connection reach every attached VPC. A virtual private gateway belongs to only one VPC, so each VPC would need its own VPN."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"Which capabilities does AWS Transit Gateway provide? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Caching static content at edge locations","Issuing SSL/TLS certificates for load balancers","Peering with transit gateways in other AWS Regions","Blocking SQL injection attacks at layer 7","Attaching VPCs, AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections, and AWS Direct Connect gateways to a central hub"],"a":[2,4],"e":"Transit Gateway connects VPCs, VPNs, and Direct Connect at a regional hub and supports inter-Region peering. Edge caching is a CloudFront feature, and SQL injection protection is AWS WAF."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A network team wants to create one AWS Transit Gateway in a shared networking account and let VPCs in other accounts of the organization attach to it. Which service should the team use to share the transit gateway?","o":["AWS Service Catalog","AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)","AWS Control Tower","AWS Firewall Manager"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS RAM shares resources such as transit gateways with other accounts or OUs, so they can attach their VPCs. Firewall Manager manages security rules across accounts but does not share network resources."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"Employees working from home need secure access from their laptops to applications in a VPC and in the company's on-premises network. Which managed AWS service should the company use?","o":["AWS Site-to-Site VPN","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Client VPN","AWS Transit Gateway"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Client VPN is a managed, client-based VPN that lets individual users connect securely from their devices. Site-to-Site VPN connects entire networks, such as a data center to a VPC, not individual users."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"What is the main difference between AWS Client VPN and AWS Site-to-Site VPN?","o":["Client VPN connects individual user devices; Site-to-Site VPN connects an entire on-premises network to AWS","Client VPN uses a dedicated private fiber line; Site-to-Site VPN uses the internet","Client VPN works only within one Availability Zone; Site-to-Site VPN is Regional","Client VPN is free; Site-to-Site VPN is billed per GB stored"],"a":[0],"e":"Client VPN is for remote users running a VPN client, while Site-to-Site VPN links a customer gateway device on a network to AWS. A dedicated private line is AWS Direct Connect, which is neither of these."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company wants remote contractors to authenticate to its VPN with their existing Active Directory or SAML identity provider credentials and receive access only to specific VPC subnets. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS PrivateLink","Amazon CloudFront signed URLs","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Client VPN"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Client VPN supports Active Directory, SAML-based federated, and certificate authentication, with authorization rules for network access. PrivateLink exposes a service privately to other VPCs; it does not give remote users a VPN."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A company must give 500 remote staff secure, encrypted access to private resources in AWS. It does not want to run and scale its own VPN servers on Amazon EC2. Which service meets this requirement?","o":["AWS Global Accelerator","AWS Client VPN","A NAT gateway","AWS Shield Standard"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Client VPN is a fully managed, elastic remote-access VPN, so there are no VPN servers to operate. A NAT gateway provides outbound internet access for private subnets, not inbound user access."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"A SaaS provider wants to expose its service to customers' VPCs without the traffic crossing the public internet and without VPC peering. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Transit Gateway","AWS PrivateLink","An internet gateway","Amazon Route 53"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS PrivateLink lets consumers reach a service through interface VPC endpoints over the AWS network, without peering or internet exposure. Transit Gateway connects whole networks, which gives far broader access than a single service."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"Two VPCs have overlapping CIDR ranges, so they cannot be peered. An application in one VPC must call one internal service in the other. Which option works?","o":["Create a VPC peering connection","Attach an internet gateway to both VPCs and use public IPs","Use AWS Client VPN between the VPCs","Expose the service with AWS PrivateLink and connect through an interface VPC endpoint"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS PrivateLink works even with overlapping CIDRs because consumers connect to an endpoint network interface in their own VPC. VPC peering does not support overlapping CIDR ranges."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"Instances in a private subnet with no internet access must call AWS service APIs, such as AWS Systems Manager, privately. What should the company create?","o":["Interface VPC endpoints powered by AWS PrivateLink","A NAT gateway in the private subnet","An AWS Direct Connect connection","An AWS Client VPN endpoint"],"a":[0],"e":"Interface VPC endpoints use AWS PrivateLink to reach supported AWS services privately from inside the VPC. A NAT gateway would send traffic toward the internet, which the requirement avoids."},
  {"d":3,"t":"network","q":"Which statements about AWS PrivateLink are correct? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Traffic between the consumer VPC and the service stays on the AWS network","It provides full transitive routing between all connected VPCs","Consumers access the service through interface VPC endpoints in their own VPC","It requires an internet gateway in the consumer VPC","It replicates data between Regions for disaster recovery"],"a":[0,2],"e":"PrivateLink provides private, one-way access to a specific service through interface endpoints, and traffic does not traverse the internet. Transitive routing between networks is what AWS Transit Gateway provides."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company wants a disaster recovery solution for its on-premises servers that keeps a low-cost staging area in AWS and only launches full-size recovery instances during a disaster. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Application Migration Service","AWS Backup","AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery continuously replicates servers into a low-cost staging area and launches recovery instances only when needed. Application Migration Service uses similar replication, but its purpose is a one-time migration, not ongoing DR."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"After ransomware encrypts its servers, a company wants to recover them to a point in time just before the attack. Which AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery capability supports this?","o":["Automatic schema conversion","Edge caching of server images","Cross-account resource sharing","Point-in-time recovery from replicated snapshots"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery keeps point-in-time recovery points so you can launch servers from a state before the corruption. Cross-account resource sharing is AWS RAM and does not restore data."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company must regularly prove that its disaster recovery plan works, without affecting replication or production servers. Which AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery feature allows this?","o":["Backup Vault Lock","Service control policies","Non-disruptive recovery drills","Migration Hub milestones"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery lets you launch drill instances in AWS at any time while replication continues. Backup Vault Lock is an AWS Backup feature that makes backups immutable; it does not test recovery."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"Which capabilities does AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery provide? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Conversion of database schemas between engines","Recovery point objectives (RPOs) of seconds through continuous block-level replication","Failback to the original source environment after the disaster is over","Cost projections for migrating an on-premises data center","Tracking of software license usage"],"a":[1,2],"e":"Elastic Disaster Recovery replicates continuously for RPOs of seconds and supports failback once the primary site is restored. Schema conversion is AWS SCT, and cost projections come from Migration Evaluator."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company runs critical applications on Amazon EC2 in one AWS Region and wants to recover them quickly into a different Region if the primary Region becomes unavailable, using continuous replication. Which service fits best?","o":["AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","AWS Global Accelerator","Amazon CloudFront","AWS Transit Gateway"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery can replicate EC2 instances across Regions and launch them in the recovery Region within minutes. Global Accelerator routes traffic to healthy endpoints but does not replicate servers or data."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company wants to apply the same backup policy to all accounts in its AWS Organizations organization from the management account. Which approach should it use?","o":["Amazon EBS snapshots created manually in each account","AWS Backup with backup policies in AWS Organizations","AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery in each account","AWS Storage Gateway Tape Gateway"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Backup integrates with Organizations so backup policies can be applied centrally to every account. Manually created snapshots in each account do not give centralized policy-based management."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"An auditor wants a report proving that backups for all resources meet the company's required frequency and retention. Which AWS Backup feature provides this?","o":["AWS Artifact","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Backup Audit Manager","AWS Health Dashboard"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Backup Audit Manager evaluates backup activity against controls you define and generates compliance reports. AWS Artifact provides AWS's own compliance reports, not reports on your backups."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company wants to back up its on-premises VMware virtual machines and its Amazon EC2 instances using one centrally managed service. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Backup","AWS Application Migration Service","AWS DataSync","AWS Migration Hub"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Backup supports VMware virtual machines on premises (and in VMware Cloud on AWS) alongside many AWS services. DataSync moves files between storage systems but is not a policy-based backup service."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"A company wants every new Amazon EFS file system and Amazon DynamoDB table tagged backup=daily to be included automatically in a daily backup plan. Which service supports this tag-based resource assignment?","o":["AWS Config","AWS Service Catalog","AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","AWS Backup"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Backup plans can select resources by tag, so new tagged resources are protected automatically. AWS Config can detect untagged resources but does not create backups."},
  {"d":3,"t":"storage","q":"Which features does AWS Backup provide? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Continuous block-level replication for an RPO of seconds","Real-time protection against DDoS attacks","Copying backups to another AWS Region or another AWS account","Backup Vault Lock to make backups immutable (write once, read many)","Automatic schema conversion during restore"],"a":[2,3],"e":"AWS Backup supports cross-Region and cross-account copies and Vault Lock for WORM protection. Continuous block-level replication with RPOs of seconds is AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery, not AWS Backup."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company needs free public SSL/TLS certificates for its Application Load Balancer and wants them renewed automatically. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS CloudHSM","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS Secrets Manager"],"a":[1],"e":"ACM provides public certificates at no additional cost for integrated services such as Elastic Load Balancing and manages renewal. AWS KMS manages encryption keys, not SSL/TLS certificates."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company imported a certificate from a third-party certificate authority into AWS Certificate Manager (ACM). Which statement is correct?","o":["ACM renews imported certificates automatically like ACM-issued ones","ACM does not renew imported certificates automatically, so the company must reimport a renewed certificate","Imported certificates cannot be used with Elastic Load Balancing","Imported certificates are stored in AWS CloudHSM"],"a":[1],"e":"ACM manages renewal only for certificates it issues; imported certificates must be renewed with the original CA and reimported. ACM does send expiration notices for imported certificates, but it does not renew them."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which AWS services can use public certificates from AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) to serve HTTPS traffic? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon CloudFront","Elastic Load Balancing","Amazon S3 Glacier","AWS Batch","Amazon EBS"],"a":[0,1],"e":"ACM integrates with CloudFront, Elastic Load Balancing, and Amazon API Gateway to terminate HTTPS. Services such as Amazon EBS do not serve HTTPS endpoints, so they do not use ACM certificates."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A team wants to enable HTTPS on its Amazon API Gateway custom domain and avoid tracking certificate expiration dates manually. Which service handles the certificate?","o":["AWS Directory Service","Amazon Cognito","AWS Firewall Manager","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)"],"a":[3],"e":"ACM provisions and automatically renews certificates for integrated services such as API Gateway custom domains. Amazon Cognito authenticates application users; it does not issue TLS certificates."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A financial company's regulators require that its encryption keys be stored in a single-tenant hardware security module that only the company controls, with no AWS access to the keys. Which service meets this requirement?","o":["AWS CloudHSM","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) with AWS managed keys","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated, single-tenant HSMs in your VPC where you exclusively manage the keys and users. AWS KMS uses multi-tenant HSMs managed by AWS, which may not satisfy a single-tenant requirement."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An application uses industry-standard cryptographic APIs such as PKCS#11 and Java Cryptography Extension (JCE) and needs a hardware security module on AWS. Which service supports these APIs?","o":["AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS CloudHSM","Amazon Macie"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS CloudHSM supports standard interfaces such as PKCS#11, JCE, and Microsoft CNG, so existing applications can use it. AWS KMS is used through its own AWS API, not PKCS#11."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to keep using AWS KMS integrations with services like Amazon S3 and Amazon EBS, but the key material must be generated and stored in HSMs that the company exclusively controls. What should it use?","o":["AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) private certificates","An IAM credential report","An AWS KMS custom key store backed by AWS CloudHSM"],"a":[3],"e":"A KMS custom key store backed by an AWS CloudHSM cluster keeps key material in your own HSMs while still working with KMS-integrated services. Secrets Manager stores secrets such as passwords; it does not hold KMS key material."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Under the shared responsibility model for AWS CloudHSM, which task is the customer's responsibility?","o":["Replacing failed HSM hardware","Patching the HSM firmware","Managing the HSM users, keys, and access to the cryptographic material","Providing physical security for the HSM appliances"],"a":[2],"e":"With CloudHSM, AWS manages the hardware, firmware, and physical security, while the customer manages HSM users and keys. Because AWS cannot access the keys, losing the credentials means the keys cannot be recovered."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A mobile app must let signed-in users upload photos directly to their own folder in an Amazon S3 bucket by using temporary, limited-privilege AWS credentials. Which Amazon Cognito component provides those credentials?","o":["Amazon Cognito identity pools","Amazon Cognito user pools","IAM users created for each app user","IAM Identity Center permission sets"],"a":[0],"e":"Cognito identity pools exchange a user's identity token for temporary AWS credentials tied to an IAM role. User pools authenticate users and issue tokens, but they do not hand out AWS credentials."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A consumer website expects millions of customers to register. Which approach is recommended for managing these customers' sign-up, sign-in, and password resets?","o":["Create an IAM user for each customer","Use an Amazon Cognito user pool","Use AWS IAM Identity Center for each customer","Store usernames and passwords in AWS Secrets Manager"],"a":[1],"e":"Amazon Cognito user pools are a managed user directory built for application end users at large scale. IAM users and IAM Identity Center are meant for people who access AWS itself, not for application customers."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A mobile app wants to let visitors browse some content before they sign up, while still giving them limited access to AWS resources. Which Amazon Cognito feature supports this?","o":["A cross-account IAM role","An IAM credential report","Unauthenticated (guest) identities in an Amazon Cognito identity pool","A Service Catalog launch constraint"],"a":[2],"e":"Cognito identity pools can issue limited temporary credentials to unauthenticated guest users. A cross-account IAM role lets principals in another AWS account access resources; it is not for anonymous app visitors."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A B2B web application must let employees of customer companies sign in with their own corporate SAML 2.0 identity providers, and the app also needs MFA for local users. Which service should the developers use?","o":["Amazon Cognito","AWS Directory Service Simple AD","AWS IAM Identity Center","AWS Client VPN"],"a":[0],"e":"Amazon Cognito user pools support federation with SAML and OIDC providers and MFA for application users. IAM Identity Center federates workforce access to AWS accounts and applications for your own organization, not customer sign-in to your app."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants a fully managed Microsoft Active Directory in AWS so it can join Windows EC2 instances to a domain and use Group Policy, without running domain controllers itself. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Cognito","AWS IAM Identity Center","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","AWS Directory Service for Microsoft Active Directory (AWS Managed Microsoft AD)"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Managed Microsoft AD runs real Microsoft Active Directory domain controllers managed by AWS. Amazon Cognito is a directory for application end users and does not support domain join or Group Policy."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants AWS applications such as Amazon WorkSpaces to authenticate users against its existing on-premises Active Directory, without storing or caching any directory data in AWS. Which option should it use?","o":["AWS Managed Microsoft AD with a new forest","AD Connector in AWS Directory Service","Amazon Cognito user pools","An IAM credential report"],"a":[1],"e":"AD Connector is a proxy that redirects directory requests to your on-premises Active Directory without caching information in the cloud. AWS Managed Microsoft AD would run a separate directory in AWS."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants its on-premises users to access resources in AWS that use Active Directory, and plans to set up a trust relationship between its on-premises AD and a managed directory in AWS. Which service supports this?","o":["AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)","AWS Directory Service","AWS Control Tower","Amazon Cognito identity pools"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Managed Microsoft AD in AWS Directory Service supports trust relationships with on-premises Active Directory domains. AWS RAM shares AWS resources between accounts and has nothing to do with directory trusts."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which AWS services commonly use AWS Directory Service for Active Directory authentication? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon WorkSpaces","Amazon Polly","AWS Batch","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon FSx for Windows File Server"],"a":[0,4],"e":"Amazon WorkSpaces and Amazon FSx for Windows File Server integrate with AWS Directory Service for Active Directory users and permissions. Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service that does not use Active Directory."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A security team wants AWS WAF rules applied automatically to every Application Load Balancer and CloudFront distribution in all accounts of its organization, including resources created in the future. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Shield Standard","AWS Control Tower Account Factory","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Firewall Manager"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Firewall Manager centrally configures WAF rules and other protections across accounts in AWS Organizations and applies them to new resources automatically. GuardDuty detects threats but does not deploy firewall rules."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to audit and enforce security group rules consistently across hundreds of VPCs in many accounts and remove overly permissive rules. Which service helps with this centrally?","o":["AWS Firewall Manager","AWS PrivateLink","AWS Transit Gateway","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Firewall Manager has security group policies that audit and enforce security group rules across accounts. Transit Gateway connects networks; it does not manage security group rules."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company subscribes to AWS Shield Advanced and wants Shield Advanced protection applied automatically to every new Elastic IP address and load balancer in all its accounts. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Shield Standard","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Firewall Manager","AWS Config"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Firewall Manager can apply Shield Advanced protections across accounts and to new resources as they appear. AWS Config can detect unprotected resources, but it does not centrally deploy protections in this way."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which statements about AWS Firewall Manager are correct? (Choose TWO.)","o":["It is a firewall that inspects traffic itself without any other service","It centrally manages firewall rules across accounts in AWS Organizations","It replaces the need for AWS Organizations","It provides free SSL/TLS certificates","It can manage AWS WAF, AWS Shield Advanced, security groups, and AWS Network Firewall policies"],"a":[1,4],"e":"Firewall Manager is a central management service that configures other protection services across an organization, and it requires AWS Organizations. The traffic inspection itself is done by services such as AWS WAF."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants several AWS accounts to launch resources into the same VPC subnets owned by a central networking account. Which service should it use to share the subnets?","o":["AWS PrivateLink","VPC peering","AWS Service Catalog","AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS RAM enables VPC sharing so participant accounts can create resources in subnets owned by another account. VPC peering connects two separate VPCs instead of sharing the same subnets."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to avoid creating duplicate copies of resources, such as Route 53 Resolver rules and License Manager configurations, in every account. Which service lets it create them once and share them with other accounts?","o":["AWS Firewall Manager","AWS Backup","AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)","AWS IAM Identity Center"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS RAM securely shares supported resources across accounts, organizational units, or an entire organization, which reduces duplication. IAM Identity Center manages user access to accounts and does not share resources."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"When AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM) is used within AWS Organizations, what can a resource owner share resources with? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Anonymous internet users","Individual Amazon Cognito user pool users","Third-party SaaS applications through API keys","Specific AWS accounts","Organizational units (OUs) or the entire organization"],"a":[3,4],"e":"AWS RAM shares resources with AWS accounts, OUs, or the whole organization (and with IAM roles and users for some resource types). It does not share resources with anonymous users or app end users."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which statement about sharing a resource with AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM) is correct?","o":["The resource stays owned by the original account, and the consumer accounts can use it according to the permissions granted","Ownership of the resource is transferred to the consumer account","A copy of the resource is created in each consumer account","The consumer account pays the owner account directly through AWS Marketplace"],"a":[0],"e":"With AWS RAM, the owning account keeps ownership and control while other accounts get access to use the resource. No copies are created, which is the main benefit over duplicating resources."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A startup has just launched a website on Amazon CloudFront and has not configured any DDoS protection. What protection does it already have?","o":["AWS Shield Advanced, which is enabled automatically for all accounts","AWS Shield Standard, which is enabled automatically at no extra cost","No DDoS protection until AWS WAF is configured","Amazon GuardDuty DDoS mitigation, which is included in the free tier"],"a":[1],"e":"Every AWS customer gets AWS Shield Standard automatically, at no additional charge, against common network and transport layer DDoS attacks. Shield Advanced is a paid subscription that must be enabled."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which types of attack does AWS Shield Standard help protect against?","o":["SQL injection in web forms","Compromised IAM access keys","Common network and transport layer (layer 3 and layer 4) DDoS attacks such as SYN floods and UDP reflection attacks","Malware on EC2 instances"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Shield Standard defends against the most common infrastructure-layer DDoS attacks. SQL injection is an application-layer attack handled by AWS WAF rules."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company subscribed to AWS Shield Advanced wants to add AWS WAF rules to its protected resources to mitigate application-layer DDoS attacks. What is a Shield Advanced benefit related to this?","o":["AWS WAF usage for resources protected by Shield Advanced is included at no additional cost","AWS WAF is replaced entirely by Shield Advanced","Shield Advanced removes the need for any monitoring","Shield Advanced protects only resources in a single AWS Region"],"a":[0],"e":"Shield Advanced includes AWS WAF and AWS Firewall Manager usage for protected resources at no extra cost. WAF is still the service that filters layer 7 requests; Shield Advanced does not replace it."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company's EC2-based game servers behind Elastic IP addresses are frequent DDoS targets. It wants enhanced detection, near real-time attack visibility, and 24/7 access to the AWS Shield Response Team (SRT). What should it use?","o":["AWS Shield Standard","AWS Firewall Manager alone","Amazon Inspector","AWS Shield Advanced"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Shield Advanced adds enhanced detection, attack diagnostics, and SRT support for resources such as Elastic IPs, load balancers, CloudFront, Global Accelerator, and Route 53. Shield Standard provides automatic baseline protection but no SRT access."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which statements correctly compare AWS Shield Standard and AWS Shield Advanced? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Shield Standard is automatic and free for all AWS customers","Shield Standard includes 24/7 access to the Shield Response Team","Shield Advanced is enabled by default on every AWS account","Shield Advanced includes cost protection against scaling charges caused by a DDoS attack","Shield Standard requires a Business Support plan"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Shield Standard is always on at no cost, while Shield Advanced is a paid subscription that adds SRT access, cost protection, and advanced detection. SRT access is not part of Shield Standard."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants the password for its Amazon RDS database to be rotated automatically every 30 days without changing the application, which retrieves the password at runtime. Which service should store the password?","o":["AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store standard parameters","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS CloudHSM"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Secrets Manager has built-in, managed rotation for Amazon RDS credentials. Parameter Store can store encrypted SecureString values but has no built-in automatic rotation."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A global application must read the same database secret in three AWS Regions and keep the copies in sync when the secret is rotated. Which service supports this natively?","o":["AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store standard parameters","AWS Secrets Manager with secret replication to other Regions","AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)","Amazon Cognito identity pools"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Secrets Manager can replicate secrets to other Regions and keeps replicas in sync with the primary. AWS RAM shares resources between accounts, not secret values between Regions."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A team needs to store a third-party API key that must be rotated regularly by a custom AWS Lambda function, with access audited in AWS CloudTrail. Which service is designed for this?","o":["AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","AWS License Manager","AWS Directory Service","AWS Secrets Manager"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Secrets Manager supports custom rotation with Lambda for any type of secret, and its API calls are logged in CloudTrail. Parameter Store calls are also logged, but it has no native rotation feature."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A small team wants to store a few encrypted configuration values, such as a non-rotating license key, at no additional storage cost. Automatic rotation is not needed. Which option is the most cost-effective?","o":["AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString standard parameters","AWS Secrets Manager secrets","AWS CloudHSM","Amazon RDS"],"a":[0],"e":"Standard SecureString parameters in Parameter Store are encrypted with AWS KMS and have no additional storage charge. Secrets Manager charges per secret per month, which is unnecessary when rotation is not required."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to store application settings in a hierarchy such as /prod/app/db-url and /dev/app/db-url and let EC2 instances read them at startup, mixing plain-text and encrypted values. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)"],"a":[2],"e":"Parameter Store supports hierarchical names and both String and SecureString parameters for configuration data. Secrets Manager is focused on secrets that need rotation and lifecycle management rather than general configuration."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which statements about AWS Secrets Manager are correct? (Choose TWO.)","o":["It is free for an unlimited number of secrets","It encrypts secrets using AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","It issues public SSL/TLS certificates","It can rotate secrets automatically on a schedule","It stores secrets only in plain text"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Secrets Manager encrypts secrets with KMS keys and supports scheduled rotation. It is billed per secret per month, and public certificates come from AWS Certificate Manager."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Developers in Account A need to manage resources in Account B. What is the recommended way to give them access?","o":["Create IAM users with access keys in Account B and share them with the developers","Share the root user credentials of Account B","Copy the developers' IAM users from Account A into Account B","Create an IAM role in Account B that trusts Account A, and let the developers assume it"],"a":[3],"e":"A cross-account IAM role gives temporary credentials and avoids long-term credentials in multiple accounts. Sharing access keys or root credentials violates least privilege and makes auditing difficult."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company hires an external auditing firm that uses its own AWS account. The company wants to grant the firm read-only access to its resources securely. What should it do?","o":["Create an IAM user for the auditor and email the password","Make the S3 buckets public for the audit period","Create an IAM role with read-only permissions that the auditor's AWS account can assume, using an external ID","Add the auditor's account to the company's AWS Organizations organization"],"a":[2],"e":"A cross-account role with an external ID lets a third party assume limited permissions with temporary credentials. Creating IAM users for outsiders creates long-term credentials that must be managed and rotated."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Employees already authenticate with the company's SAML 2.0 identity provider. What does federating these identities with AWS provide? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Each employee automatically receives a permanent IAM access key","AWS stores the employees' corporate passwords","Employees can access AWS without separate IAM user credentials","Federation disables MFA for all users","Access to AWS uses temporary security credentials obtained by assuming IAM roles"],"a":[2,4],"e":"With SAML federation, users sign in with their existing corporate identity and receive temporary credentials through an IAM role. No IAM users or long-term access keys are needed, and AWS does not store the corporate passwords."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company has 40 AWS accounts and wants workforce users to sign in once with the corporate identity provider and choose which account and permission set to use. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS IAM Identity Center","Amazon Cognito user pools","AWS Directory Service Simple AD","AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)"],"a":[0],"e":"IAM Identity Center provides single sign-on for workforce users to multiple AWS accounts with permission sets and can federate with an external identity provider. Cognito user pools are for customers of your applications."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An application running on an Amazon EC2 instance needs to read objects from an S3 bucket. What is the most secure way to give it credentials?","o":["Store an IAM user's access keys in the application's configuration file","Attach an IAM role to the instance through an instance profile","Embed the root user's access keys in the code","Store the access keys in a public S3 bucket"],"a":[1],"e":"An IAM role attached to the instance provides automatically rotated temporary credentials to the application. Storing long-term access keys in files or code risks leaking them."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A security team wants to find IAM users whose access keys have not been rotated in over 90 days or have never been used. Which IAM feature provides this information for all users in one file?","o":["AWS CloudTrail Insights","An AWS Config conformance pack","The IAM credential report","The AWS Health Dashboard"],"a":[2],"e":"The IAM credential report lists every IAM user with the status, last rotation date, and last used date of passwords and access keys. CloudTrail Insights detects unusual API activity patterns but does not list credential ages."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Before attaching a restrictive service control policy (SCP) to an organizational unit, an administrator wants to see which AWS services the accounts in that OU have actually used recently. Which feature should the administrator use?","o":["Service last accessed information for the organization in the IAM console","AWS Budgets reports","The AWS Organizations consolidated bill","AWS Trusted Advisor service limit checks"],"a":[0],"e":"IAM can show service last accessed information for an organization, OU, or account, which helps you write SCPs that do not block services still in use. The consolidated bill shows costs, not access patterns."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to automatically find unused IAM roles, unused access keys, and unused permissions across its accounts so it can remove them. Which feature should it use?","o":["Amazon Inspector","Amazon Macie","AWS Shield Advanced","IAM Access Analyzer unused access findings"],"a":[3],"e":"IAM Access Analyzer can generate findings for unused roles, access keys, passwords, and permissions. Amazon Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities, not unused IAM access."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A security team wants to identify S3 buckets, KMS keys, and IAM roles in its account that are shared with principals outside its AWS organization. Which feature should it use?","o":["The IAM credential report","IAM Access Analyzer external access findings","AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM) resource shares only","AWS Artifact"],"a":[1],"e":"IAM Access Analyzer analyzes resource policies to find resources that grant access to external principals. The credential report only covers IAM users' credential status, not resource sharing."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which IAM features help an administrator apply the principle of least privilege by revealing permissions that are not being used? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Last accessed information for users, roles, and groups","AWS Shield Standard","IAM Access Analyzer unused access findings","Amazon Cognito user pools","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Last accessed information shows when identities last used each service, and Access Analyzer reports unused access, so unused permissions can be removed. Cognito user pools handle application sign-in and do not analyze IAM permissions."},
];
